<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tang Jingsong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1841–1903</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tang Jingsong was a Qing official from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> who was governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> ceded the island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1895. Local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> made him president of the short-lived Republic of Formosa, proclaimed on 25 May 1895 in the hope of foreign intervention. When Japanese troops landed he fled to the mainland within days, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taipei">Taipei</a> fell in early June.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tang-jingsong.jpg" alt="Tang Jingsong" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%99%9A%E5%B9%B4%E5%94%90%E6%99%AF%E5%B4%A7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
